        "category": "correctness",
        "consequence": "A deployment that is meant to run FIPS can silently end up on the non-FIPS provider, or the reverse, after a classpath change. The old fail-fast check that caught this misconfiguration is gone.",
        "evidence_refs": [
          "source:new:common/src/main/java/org/keycloak/common/crypto/CryptoIntegration.java@sha256:88dfbd12eace63d912ac9d98eeee1e1d39e556df811566f2705056d46aa68b6d",
          "source:new:crypto/default/src/main/java/org/keycloak/crypto/def/DefaultCryptoProvider.java@sha256:3b2da442a772c65a177d6d2c4f4fc75bbf3adf33bc1741d1ff955f978b486673",
          "source:new:crypto/elytron/src/main/java/org/keycloak/crypto/elytron/WildFlyElytronProvider.java@sha256:55ddf0898a799c676540b0d064ab4f8fd7e25850a98d65a731ab55dffe0ee537",
          "source:old:common/src/main/java/org/keycloak/common/crypto/CryptoIntegration.java@sha256:1ecbf724f414e735ef7151941f0d6d2750dbf1c1d16f88f8c7211ec223d79ed9"
        ],
        "explanation": "detectProvider used to throw when more than one CryptoProvider was found. It now sorts by order() and takes the first one. DefaultCryptoProvider, WildFlyElytronProvider and FIPS1402Provider all return 200, so they tie. Collection sorting is stable, so a tie is broken by ServiceLoader discovery order, which follows the classpath or module order. If a FIPS provider and the default BouncyCastle provider are both present, either one can be chosen. The only trace is a debug log line.",
        "lines": {
